  "body": "## The problem\n\nA fallback chain could only fire on an **exception**. A model that accepted the request and then went silent raised nothing, so the only thing that ever ended the attempt was the transport read timeout — 300s later — after which the *same stalled model* was retried.\n\nMeasured on a real 51,000-request log:\n\n- 4 requests reached their fallback after **566s on average**, all cancelled by the client first\n- 190 stalled requests burned **25.7 hours** of wall clock\n\nThe chain was picking the right model. It was picking it nine minutes too late.\n\n## The fix\n\nSlow is not the same as failed. Three limits, all disabled by setting them to `0`:\n\n| Setting | Default | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `FALLBACK_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT` | 120s | Nothing has reached the client before the first chunk, so handing over is invisible |\n| `FALLBACK_TOTAL_TIMEOUT` | 600s | Backstop for a stream that committed and then stalled — no chain can rescue it, but it should stop |\n| `FALLBACK_EJECT_AFTER_FAILURES` / `FALLBACK_EJECT_SECONDS` | 3 / 30s | Skip a model that just failed repeatedly instead of re-paying its timeout |\n\n## Where the defaults come from\n\nReplayed against the log rather than picked:\n\n```\nfirst token, 51,118 successful requests:  p50 4.5s   p90 8.9s   p99 38.9s   p99.9 181.7s\n  120s deadline -> 0.209% of healthy requests re-rolled onto the next model\n\nwhole request, 51,549 successful:         p50 7.5s   p90 21.0s  p99 99.8s   p99.9 255.7s\n  600s budget   -> 0.025% cut short\n```\n\nA re-roll is not a lost request: it is one extra hop, paid at the next model's own TTFT (p50 4.5s).\n\n## Safety\n\n- **Ejection never empties a chain.** If every model on a route is benched, the chain is tried in order anyway — skipping a bad model is an optimisation, refusing to try anything is an outage.\n- **A provider's own timeout is not misreported as ours.** `asyncio.wait` rather than `wait_for`, so a `TimeoutError` raised by the provider stays distinguishable from this deadline elapsing.\n- **The commit boundary is unchanged.** Once bytes are on the wire no fallback runs, so two models can still never be spliced into one reply.\n\n## Tests\n\n15 new tests covering: hand-over on first-token deadline, the deadline *not* applying to a slow-but-producing stream, committed stalls ending at the budget, the chain not being extended once the budget is spent, limits disabled, upstream timeouts not misreported, ejection skipping a model, the all-ejected safety net, and a success clearing the streak.\n\nNote: 50 tests fail identically on `main` and on this branch in a local Windows checkout (websearch, installers, web-tools) — this branch introduces none of them.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
