     "date": "2026-08-09T17:18:50Z"
    },
    "message": "feat(routing): give a stalled model a deadline so the chain can take over\n\nA fallback chain could only fire on an exception. A model that accepted the\nrequest and then went silent raised nothing, so the only thing that ever ended\nthe attempt was the transport read timeout -- 300s later, after which the same\nstalled model was retried. On a real log four such requests reached their\nfallback after 566s on average, by which point the client had given up, and\n190 stalls burned 25.7 hours of wall clock in total.\n\nSlow is not the same as failed, and treating the two alike is what turned a\nlatency problem into an unreachable chain.\n\n- FALLBACK_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT (120s): nothing has reached the client before\n  the first chunk, so abandoning the attempt there is invisible and the next\n  model simply answers. Measured against 51,118 successful requests: first\n  token is 4.5s at p50 and 181.7s at p99.9, so 120s re-rolls 0.21% of healthy\n  requests onto the next model and ends stalls that ran for minutes.\n- FALLBACK_TOTAL_TIMEOUT (600s): a whole-request budget for the case no chain\n  can rescue -- a stream that committed and then stalled. Whole-request\n  durations are 7.5s at p50 and 255.7s at p99.9, so 600s cuts 0.03% short.\n- FALLBACK_EJECT_AFTER_FAILURES / FALLBACK_EJECT_SECONDS (3 / 30s): routing\n  skips a provider/model that just failed repeatedly, so a request stops\n  re-paying a dead model's timeout on its way to a healthy fallback. If every\n  model on a route is benched the chain is tried in order anyway: skipping a\n  bad model is an optimisation, refusing to try anything is an outage.\n\nThe deadline uses asyncio.wait rather than wait_for so a TimeoutError raised by\nthe provider stays distinguishable from this deadline elapsing; the two mean\ndifferent things and only one of them is our decision.\n\nEvery limit is disabled by setting it to 0.\n\nMINOR: new config options and behaviour additions, backward compatible.",
